  "query_preview": "..The latest satellite imagery shows a fairly extensive \ncloud shield north of a Nogales to Lordsburg New Mexico line, with \ngenerally clear skies south of this line attm. The KEMX radar \nindicates spotty light showers/sprinkles across the eastern portion \nof this cloud shield from Three Points eastward, and scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms over portions of central and \nwestern Pima County. Radar estimated precipitation amounts with the \nstrongest storms over the western half of Pima County are currently \nin the 0.20 to 0.40 inch per hour range. \n\nThese showers and thunderstorms were in response to a mid level \ndisturbance residing over far northwest Sonora and southwestern \nArizona this morning. The models lift this disturbance slowly north \nto northeast across Arizona later today. The high res solutions \npoint to the showers/thunderstorms winding down across western Pima \nCounty by the late morning hours, with isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms developing during the mid to late afternoon hours \nroughly east of a Nogales to Eloy line. The high res models then \nlift these showers and thunderstorms to the northeast across the \neastern portion of the forecast area through the evening hours \ntonight. This evolution matches up well with our current gridded \nforecast.  \n\nOtherwise, PWATS currently range from 0.80 inches in the White \nMountains to near 1.50 inches across far western Pima County. PWATs \nwill hold steady in the 1.0 to 1.3 inch range through late tonight \nbefore starting to lower on Friday. The main threat from the storms \ntoday will be heavy rainfall with an isolated flash flooding threat. \nHigh temperatures will top out 1-3 degrees below normal today. \n\nOverall, the forecast looks on track. No updates this morning to the \ngrids or text products. For more information on the latter periods \nof the forecast, please refer to the PREV DISCUSSION section below.",
